{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>To appoint for the mourners of Zion.</b> It is what He explained: beauty instead of ashes, oil of gladness instead of mourning, for a mourner does not anoint with oil, and the woman of Tekoa before him (2 Samuel 14:2):" "<b>A faint spirit.</b> An adjective noun from the derivation of כהה הנגע (Leviticus 13:6). Some say it is like the meaning of darkness, but the correct one in my eyes is that it is the opposite of פשה." "<b>Oaks of righteousness.</b> From the derivation of trees, like מאילים אשר חמדתם (Isaiah 1:29), and the witness is מטע השם."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 61, :notes {2 "The phrase מטע השם ('the planting of YHWH') appears in the same verse (Isaiah 61:3), serving as Ibn Ezra's textual witness that אילי refers to trees; it is retained in Hebrew as a cited phrase."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:00:35.643616314Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}